    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 tests green).\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved all date-derived owner values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, labelled by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 the Australian convention consistent with this app's AU regions/timezones).\n\n**New helper** \u2014 `mapper/FiscalYear.java`:\n- `endingYear(date)` \u2014 the fiscal year a date falls in (month \u2265 July \u2192 next calendar year).\n- `label(owner)` \u2192 `\"FY<YY>\"` from the owner's business-day-adjusted `registrationDate` (null-safe).\n- `yearSegment(date)` \u2014 the two-digit `YY`, shared by the label and the membership number.\n- `elapsedYears(owner)` \u2014 whole fiscal years (1-July boundaries) elapsed since registration.\n\n**Wiring the change through:**\n- `OwnerMapper` \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` mapping; the `membershipNumber` year segment now uses `FiscalYear.yearSegment(...)` instead of `registrationDate.getYear() % 100`.\n- `MembershipLevel` \u2014 tenure points now trigger on `FiscalYear.elapsedYears(owner) > 1` (more than one elapsed fiscal year) rather than a 365-day window; removed the now-unused day-count code.\n- `AuditOwnerCreated` \u2014 its inline membership-number string uses the same fiscal-year segment, keeping the audit log consistent with the DTO.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property (source of the generated `OwnerDto.fiscalYear`) and updated the `membershipNumber`/`membershipPoints` descriptions.\n\nSince `registrationDate` is stored already business-day-adjusted (rolled off weekends/holidays by `ResolveRegistrationDate`/`DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate`), every fiscal-year value derives from that adjusted date. The audit output confirms it end-to-end: input `2026-08-15` (a Saturday) rolls to Monday `2026-08-17`, which is in the fiscal year ending 2027 \u2192 `membershipNumber=...-M27` and `fiscalYear=FY27`.\n\nOne note on a spec ambiguity I had to resolve: `FY<YY>` uses the **ending** year (a Jul-2026 date \u2192 `FY27`). The acceptance test only pins the `FY\\d{2}` format, so if you intended the *starting* year instead, that's a one-line change in `FiscalYear.endingYear`.",
